# Naive Embodied-SlotSSM Reimplementation

🤖 **Object-centric temporal memory for long-horizon robotic manipulation**

An unofficial PyTorch reimplementation of **Embodied-SlotSSM**, an object-centric temporal-memory framework for long-horizon robotic manipulation introduced in:

> **Rethinking Progression of Memory State in Robotic Manipulation: An Object-Centric Perspective** (AAAI 2026)

🌐 [Project page](https://libero-mem.github.io/) · 📄 [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11478) · 💾 [Dataset repository](https://github.com/libero-mem/libero-mem) · 🤗 [Dataset on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/datasets/libero-mem/LIBERO-Mem)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This repository is an experimental reimplementation, not the authors' official release. The training scripts still contain development assumptions, including checkpoint paths that must be adapted to your environment. See [Known limitations](#known-limitations) before starting a run.

## 🧠 Overview

Conventional vision-language-action (VLA) models represent each frame with hundreds of dense visual tokens. Embodied-SlotSSM instead compresses a scene into object-centric slots and models how those slots evolve over time.

This repository builds on an OpenVLA backbone and organizes training into three stages:

| Stage | Objective | Input checkpoint | Output checkpoint |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 🧩 1. Slot learning | Learn object slots, masks, boxes, objectness, and interactability | Initial slot weights | `pretrained_slots-wo-filters_s16.safetensors` |
| ⏳ 2. Temporal pretraining | Reconstruct past slots and forecast future slots with SlotSSM | Stage 1 | `object_centric_bwd25_fwd6.safetensors` |
| 🦾 3. Action prediction | Decode continuous actions from language-conditioned slot memory | Stage 2 | `object_centric_bwd25+fwd6_actionable.safetensors` |

```text
RGB-D trajectory + language
             │
             ▼
  🧩 Stage 1: object-centric slots
             │
             ▼
  ⏳ Stage 2: temporal SlotSSM
             │
             ▼
  🦾 Stage 3: continuous actions
```

## 🗂️ Repository layout

```text
.
├── vla-scripts/
│   ├── finetune-1_slotvla_slot-wo-filter.py
│   ├── finetune-2_slotssm_pretrain.py
│   └── finetune-3_slotssm_to_action.py
├── prismatic/          # OpenVLA model, data, loss, and training modules
├── dlimp_openvla/     # RLDS input-pipeline package
├── scripts/            # Pretraining, generation, and preprocessing utilities
├── experiments/        # Experiment configuration
└── figures/            # Dataset/task visualizations
```

## ✅ Requirements

- Linux
- Python 3.10 or later
- A CUDA-capable GPU
- CUDA 11.8 or later
- PyTorch 2.1 or later
- Enough local storage for OpenVLA weights, LIBERO-Mem, and checkpoints

Exact GPU memory requirements depend on the stage, batch size, horizon, enabled modalities, and whether LoRA or quantization is used.

## 🛠️ Installation

Create and activate an isolated environment:

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
```

Install PyTorch using the command appropriate for your CUDA version from the [PyTorch installation guide](https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/), then install the remaining dependencies:

```bash
pip install transformers accelerate peft safetensors draccus \
    tqdm matplotlib scipy numpy wandb tensorboard
pip install -e ./dlimp_openvla
```

The bundled `dlimp_openvla` package pins TensorFlow 2.15 and installs TensorFlow Datasets. Depending on your dataset transforms, additional packages such as `tensorflow-graphics` may be required.

## 💾 Dataset

The training pipeline expects an RLDS/TFDS-formatted dataset. For LIBERO-Mem, arrange the data as:

```text
<data_root_dir>/
└── <dataset_name>/
    └── 1.0.0/
```

For example:

```text
/data/LIBERO-Mem/
└── libero_mem/
    └── 1.0.0/
```

Samples are expected to include RGB or RGB-D observations, language instructions, segmentation maps, object metadata and bounding boxes, tracking information, and object-interaction annotations. Stage 3 additionally uses action supervision.

## 🚀 Training

All stages use [`torchrun`](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/elastic/run.html). Run commands from the repository root.

The examples below use one GPU and the `25`-step backward plus `6`-step forward configuration. For Stages 2 and 3, `horizon_size` should equal `bwd_steps + fwd_steps + 1` (32 in this example).

### 🧩 Stage 1: object-centric slot learning

Stage 1 fine-tunes object-centric representations with segmentation-aware supervision. You may initialize these components from a compatible checkpoint with `--load_slot_path`. If the option is omitted or the path does not exist, the object-centric modules use their default initialization.

```bash
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 torchrun \
    --standalone \
    --nnodes 1 \
    --nproc-per-node 1 \
    vla-scripts/finetune-1_slotvla_slot-wo-filter.py \
    --vla_path openvla/openvla-7b \
    --data_root_dir /data/LIBERO-Mem \
    --dataset_name libero_mem \
    --run_root_dir ./runs \
    --adapter_tmp_dir ./checkpoints \
    --output_dir ./weights \
    --batch_size 16 \
    --horizon_size 36 \
    --number_of_slots 16 \
    --learning_rate 1e-5 \
    --grad_accumulation_steps 1 \
    --save_steps 250 \
    --image_aug True \
    --modality_key 1000
```

The script writes `pretrained_slots-wo-filters_s16.safetensors` below `output_dir`.

### ⏳ Stage 2: SlotSSM temporal pretraining

Stage 2 freezes the learned object-centric components and trains temporal backward reconstruction and forward prediction.

```bash
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 torchrun \
    --standalone \
    --nnodes 1 \
    --nproc-per-node 1 \
    vla-scripts/finetune-2_slotssm_pretrain.py \
    --vla_path openvla/openvla-7b \
    --data_root_dir /data/LIBERO-Mem \
    --dataset_name libero_mem \
    --run_root_dir ./runs \
    --adapter_tmp_dir ./checkpoints \
    --batch_size 16 \
    --horizon_size 32 \
    --bwd_steps 25 \
    --fwd_steps 6 \
    --number_of_slots 16 \
    --learning_rate 2e-5 \
    --grad_accumulation_steps 1 \
    --save_steps 5000 \
    --image_aug True \
    --modality_key 1000
```

Before running this stage, connect the Stage 1 checkpoint as described in [Known limitations](#known-limitations). Checkpoints are saved below `<adapter_tmp_dir>/<experiment_name>/`.

### 🦾 Stage 3: embodied action prediction

Stage 3 loads the temporal representation and trains the actionable policy, primarily through LoRA-based adaptation and continuous-action supervision.

```bash
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 torchrun \
    --standalone \
    --nnodes 1 \
    --nproc-per-node 1 \
    vla-scripts/finetune-3_slotssm_to_action.py \
    --vla_path openvla/openvla-7b \
    --data_root_dir /data/LIBERO-Mem \
    --dataset_name libero_mem \
    --run_root_dir ./runs \
    --adapter_tmp_dir ./checkpoints \
    --load_slot_path /path/to/object_centric_bwd25_fwd6.safetensors \
    --lora_rank 64 \
    --batch_size 6 \
    --horizon_size 32 \
    --bwd_steps 25 \
    --fwd_steps 6 \
    --number_of_slots 16 \
    --learning_rate 1e-5 \
    --grad_accumulation_steps 1 \
    --save_steps 1000 \
    --image_aug True \
    --modality_key 1000
```

Before running this stage, note that the current implementation does not yet read the checkpoint from `--load_slot_path`; see [Known limitations](#known-limitations).

### ⚡ Multi-GPU training

Expose the desired devices and set `--nproc-per-node` to their count:

```bash
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1,2,3 torchrun \
    --standalone \
    --nnodes 1 \
    --nproc-per-node 4 \
    vla-scripts/finetune-2_slotssm_pretrain.py \
    <stage-2-options>
```

The configured `batch_size` is per process. The effective batch size is:

```text
batch_size × grad_accumulation_steps × number_of_processes
```

## 👁️ Observation modalities

`--modality_key` is a four-character bit mask:

```text
[main RGB, wrist RGB, main depth, wrist depth]
```

| Key | Enabled observations |
| --- | --- |
| `1000` | Main RGB |
| `1100` | Main RGB and wrist RGB |
| `0010` | Main depth |
| `1111` | All four modalities |

Keep the value quoted if a shell or configuration layer might interpret leading zeroes.

## ⚠️ Known limitations

- Stage 1 can start without `load_slot_path`, but a compatible pretrained slot checkpoint may improve convergence.
- Stage 2 currently calls `vla.load_state_dict(weights, ...)` without defining or loading `weights`. Add a configurable checkpoint load using the Stage 1 output before running it.
- Stage 3 declares `load_slot_path`, but currently loads Stage 2 weights from a hard-coded `/cm/shared/...` path. Replace that load with `load_file(cfg.load_slot_path)` for your environment.
- The scripts are research-oriented and may include debugging helpers or incomplete branches.
- There is currently no automated evaluation or inference workflow documented in this repository.

